---
// /privacy notice (2026-11-10, Security review blocker 1, UK GDPR Art 13/14).
// Every statement mirrors what the code does or is built to do; keep it in
// step with backend/src/rateLimit.ts (daily IP purge), backend/wrangler.toml
// (invocation_logs = false), the Cloudflare Web Analytics beacon in
// layouts/Layout.astro, and, once merged, workers/outreach (emails, replies,
// suppression, retention purge) and workers/policy-watcher (policy snapshots).
// Do NOT add a claim that PolicyPeek is registered with the ICO: the data
// protection fee has not been paid (Elliot, 2026-11-10).
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
const title = "Privacy notice";
const description = "What personal data PolicyPeek handles (site, extension, emails to companies' privacy teams and policy-page snapshots), why, how long it's kept, and your rights.";
const updated = "10 November 2026";
---
<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Privacy notice</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This page explains what personal data PolicyPeek handles, why, who else
    handles it, how long it's kept, and what you can do about it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Last updated: {updated}</em></p>

  <h2 id="who">Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    PolicyPeek is a free, independent checker of whether AI and software tools
    use customer data to train AI models, based on each tool's own published
    policies. It is run by Elliot Inkley in the United Kingdom, who is the
    controller of the personal data described here.
  </p>
  <p>
    Contact for anything on this page, including requests and objections:
    <a href="mailto:hello@getpolicypeek.com">hello@getpolicypeek.com</a>.
    If we have emailed you from privacy@getpolicypeek.com, you can also simply
    reply to that email.
  </p>

  <h2 id="site">Using the website and browser extension</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>There are no accounts and no advertising, and the site sets no cookies.</li>
    <li>
      The site uses Cloudflare Web Analytics to count page views. It doesn't use
      cookies and doesn't build a profile of you.
    </li>
    <li>
      The extension only reads the address of the tab you're on when you click
      its icon. To look up a badge it sends that site's domain name (for example
      <code>example.com</code>) to <code>api.getpolicypeek.com</code>. If you
      press "Check this site", it also sends that page's address without its
      query string. Recent results are stored on your device only.
    </li>
    <li>
      When you ask us to check a new tool (on the site or in the extension), we
      record your IP address (for IPv6, its network part) and the date, so we can
      allow one free check per day. These records are deleted every day; nothing
      older than the current day is kept. The check itself (the tool's domain,
      its policy address and what we found) isn't linked to you.
    </li>
    <li>
      Our server is set not to store per-request logs, so request details such as
      IP addresses aren't kept in our logs.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="outreach">Emails to companies' privacy teams</h2>
  <p>
    When a tool's published policy doesn't say clearly whether customer data
    trains AI models, we may email the privacy, data protection, legal or press
    contact that the company publishes in that policy, from
    privacy@getpolicypeek.com, to ask. We send one email and, if there is no
    reply, at most one follow-up 14 days later. Then we stop.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What we hold:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      the contact address the company published (for example privacy@company.com),
      the page we found it on, the company and tool name, and the policy we reviewed;
    </li>
    <li>
      a record of each email we sent: the date, whether it was the first email or
      the follow-up, and the message reference our email provider gives it;
    </li>
    <li>
      any reply: the full message as received (up to about 256 KB; for attachments
      we keep only their names, types and sizes), the sender's address, the date,
      and the email authentication results (SPF, DKIM and DMARC);
    </li>
    <li>
      if you ask us not to email again, or an email bounces: the address or domain,
      the reason and the date (see "How to object" below).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Where it comes from:</strong> the contact address comes from the
    company's own published privacy policy or notice. Anything else comes from
    the emails themselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Why, and our lawful basis:</strong> we rely on legitimate interests
    (UK GDPR Article 6(1)(f)). Our interest is publishing an accurate, sourced
    description of whether a company uses customer data to train AI models,
    instead of guessing. We write only to an address the company published for
    privacy, data protection, legal or press questions, we send at most two
    emails, and we stop as soon as you ask. These emails are enquiries, not
    marketing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What we publish:</strong> we may quote a company's written answer
    on that tool's PolicyPeek page. We don't publish the name or email address
    of the person who replied.
  </p>

  <h2 id="watcher">Snapshots of published policy pages</h2>
  <p>
    To notice when a listed tool changes its policy, we regularly fetch the public
    policy pages linked on each tool's PolicyPeek page. Our fetcher identifies
    itself as <code>PolicyPeek-PolicyWatch/1.0</code>, follows each site's
    robots.txt, and fetches one page at a time per site. We store the page text,
    a fingerprint of it, and a record of what changed. These are companies'
    published documents. If one names a person (for example a data protection
    officer), that name is in our copy too. We keep the latest 10 versions of each
    page, and change records for up to 24 months. A change is reviewed by a
    person; it never changes a rating by itself.
  </p>

  <h2 id="recipients">Who else handles this data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts the site, the API, our databases and file
      storage, and sends and receives our email. Its email service may keep a
      preview of a sent message for about seven days for delivery checks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Proton</strong> provides the mailbox that replies to
      privacy@getpolicypeek.com and hello@getpolicypeek.com are forwarded to.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Anthropic</strong> receives the text of public policy pages we check,
      so that facts can be extracted from it. It doesn't receive your IP address.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We may use AI tools to help us read a company's reply when we review its
    answer. A person decides what, if anything, changes on PolicyPeek; nothing
    is published automatically from a reply.
  </p>
  <p>We don't sell personal data, and we don't share it with advertisers or data brokers.</p>
  <p>
    Some of these providers may process data outside the UK. Where they do, they
    rely on the safeguards UK law requires, such as the UK International Data
    Transfer Addendum or an adequacy decision.
  </p>

  <h2 id="retention">How long we keep it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>IP addresses for the free daily check: deleted every day.</li>
    <li>
      Emails we sent and replies we received: deleted 12 months after the tool's
      listing is no longer on hold, or 24 months after our first email,
      whichever comes first. This is done automatically.
    </li>
    <li>
      "Don't email us" and bounce records: kept for as long as we send these
      emails, so that we never email that address or company again. They hold only
      the address or domain, the reason and the date.
    </li>
    <li>Policy page snapshots: the latest 10 versions of each page; change records up to 24 months.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You have the right to ask for a copy of the personal data we hold about you,
    to have it corrected or deleted, to restrict how we use it, and to object to
    how we use it. To make a request, email
    <a href="mailto:hello@getpolicypeek.com">hello@getpolicypeek.com</a>. We'll
    reply within one month. Because the site and extension don't use accounts or
    identifiers, we may not be able to find records about you there, and IP
    records are gone within a day anyway.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you're unhappy with how we've handled your data, please tell us first.
    You can also complain to the Information Commissioner's Office (ICO), the UK
    data protection regulator:
    <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/">ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint</a>,
    or 0303 123 1113.
  </p>

  <h2 id="object">How to object or stop our emails</h2>
  <p>
    Reply "stop" to any email from privacy@getpolicypeek.com, or write to
    <a href="mailto:hello@getpolicypeek.com">hello@getpolicypeek.com</a>. We'll
    add your address (and, if you ask, your company's domain) to a do-not-contact
    list, cancel any follow-up, and never email it again. If we're unsure whether
    a reply is an objection, we treat it as one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this notice changes in a way that matters, we'll update the date at the
    top and say what changed.
  </p>
</Layout>
